## Method and reasoning
At any step a vertex is ready exactly when its remaining indegree is zero. Choose the smallest ready vertex, which minimizes the first position at which a different valid order could differ. Removing it makes its outgoing edges disappear. If the heap empties early, the remaining subgraph has a cycle.

## Walk through the sample
Initially 1, 2 and 5 are ready. Taking 1 then 2 releases 4, which precedes 5 in the heap. Taking 4 releases 3, producing 1, 2, 4, 3, 5. Sorting only the initial frontier would miss this order.

## Complexity
Time O(m+n log n), space O(n+m).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, m;
    cin >> n >> m;
    vector<vector<int>> g(n + 1);
    vector<int> incoming(n + 1), answer;
    while (m--) {
        int u, v;
        cin >> u >> v;
        g[u].push_back(v);
        ++incoming[v];
    }
    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> open;
    for (int v = 1; v <= n; ++v)
        if (!incoming[v])
            open.push(v);
    while (!open.empty()) {
        int v = open.top();
        open.pop();
        answer.push_back(v);
        for (int u : g[v])
            if (!--incoming[u])
                open.push(u);
    }
    if (int(answer.size()) != n) {
        cout << "IMPOSSIBLE\n";
        return 0;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)
        cout << answer[i] << (i + 1 == n ? '\n' : ' ');
}
